odule":"multidaytimeselector","descriptionText":"Use for administrative purpose only.<br/>NOT recommended for saving hotspot detections as historical reference.","noGroup":true},{"id":"multidayselectorsimple","isTopModule":true,"label":"Select basic days","menuLabel":"Quick basic date selector","menuDescription":"Provides easy way to quickly select dates","module":"multidayselectorsimple","descriptionText":"Use for administrative purpose only.<br/>NOT recommended for saving hotspot detections as historical reference.","noGroup":true},{"id":"filterlayers","label":"Filter Layers","skipMenuDisplay":"true","module":"filterlayers","descriptionText":"Filter Layers and modules for additional features.","noGroup":true},{"id":"basic-fires","label":"Fires / Hotspots","menuLabel":"Basic Fires / Hotspots Display","menuDescription":"<p>Daily NRT/RT active fire detection data acquired by multiple polar-orbiting satellite sensors.</p><p>Commonly used active fire data derived from polar orbiting satellite sensors for the user selected time period with additional capabilities to visualize fire data based on their attributes.</p><p><b>\'Simple\'</b> allows basic visualization of active fire data locations,</p><p><b>\'Time-Based\'</b> displays fires coded by their time since detection relative to the present.</p>","module":"basic_fires","tag":"quick_fires","opened":true,"localRedirect":"https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov","defaultLayers":["fires_all"],"layer_refs":[{"id":"fires_all"}]},{"id":"advanced-fires","label":"Fires / Hotspots","menuLabel":"Advanced Fires / Hotspots Display","module":"advanced_fires","tag":"fires","menuDescription":"<p>Daily NRT/RT active fire detection data acquired by multiple polar-orbiting and geostationary satellite sensors. Includes user-defined legend options for visualizing active fire detection attributes (time since detection, detection confidence, fire radiative power).</p>","opened":true,"usePresetLayers":false,"localRedirect":"https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov","defaultLayers":["fires_modis_terra","fires_modis_aqua","fires_viirs_snpp","fires_viirs_noaa20","fires_viirs_noaa21","fires_landsat_landsat"],"groupBreakers":[{"id":"group1","label":"Polar Orbiting","opened":true,"layers":["fires_landsat_landsat","fires_viirs_noaa20","fires_viirs_noaa21","fires_viirs_snpp","fires_modis_aqua","fires_modis_terra"],"highlight":"recommended","menuDescription":"<p>Daily active fire detection data acquired by polar orbiting satellite sensors used in FIRMS. These satellites operate in a relatively low orbit and constantly circle the Earth passing over or near both poles while the planet rotates under them. Sensors on polar orbiting satellites  generally have swath widths ranging from 2,300km to 3,000km  providing two observations daily (daytime and nighttime) for most areas on the Earth\'s surface and provide data at relatively high spatial resolution.</p><p>Polar orbiting data are real-time (RT), within 1 hour, or near real-time (NRT) within 3 hours of satellite observation. Two daily observations are typically provided by each individual satellite sensor in the mid-latitudes (between 30 degrees north and south latitude). Additional observations are possible in the higher latitudes, up to 8 times near the poles, due to overlap of adjacent swaths.</p><p>Capabilities are provided to visualize these fire data based on their attributes. ‘Day’ and \'Night’ filters the data to display fires identified on daytime or nighttime overpasses. <br/><b>\'Simple\'</b> allows basic visualization of active fire data locations, <br/><b>\'Time-Based\'</b> displays fires coded by their time since detection relative to the present and <br/><b>\'Custom\'</b> allows active fire detection to be displayed by detection confidence or estimated fire radiative power.</p>"},{"id":"group2","label":"Geostationary","layers":["fires_goes_g18","fires_goes_g18frp","fires_goes_g19","fires_goes_g19frp","fires_goes_Him9","fires_goes_Met9","fires_goes_Met10"],"highlight":"beta","menuDescription":"<p>Active fire detection data derived from high temporal geostationary satellite sensors.</p><p>Daily RT active fire detection derived from geostationary satellite sensors. These satellites orbit at a very high altitude over a fixed point on the Earth\'s surface at the equator. They follow the daily rotation of the planet and the onboard sensors provide a full hemispheric view of the Earth centered on that fixed location at high temporal frequencies (multiple times per hour). The view geometry and curvature of the Earth result in distortion and geolocational accuracy effects in the imagery and active fire products with increasing distance in all directions from nadir.</p><p>Data are coarser spatial resolution than polar orbiting fire data, but acquired at intervals of 10-15 minutes and available within 30 minutes of observation. Data are highly filtered based on detection confidence to remove as many potential false positives as possible.</p><p>Capabilities are provided to visualize these fire data based on their attributes. ‘Day’ and \'Night’ filters the data to display fires identified on daytime or nighttime overpasses. \'Time Since Detection\' displays fires coded by their time since detection relative to the present. Non-GOES active fire data can also be displayed by detection confidence or estimated fire radiative power.</p>"},{"id":"group3","label":"NRT and Standard (for research)","layers":["fires_viirs_noaa20_nrt","fires_viirs_snpp_nrt","fires_modis_aqua_nrt","fires_modis_terra_nrt","fires_viirs_noaa20_sp","fires_viirs_snpp_sp","fires_modis_aqua_sp","fires_modis_terra_sp"],"menuDescription":"<p>This layer enables a comparison between Near Real-Time (NRT) and Standard fire products.</p><p>- NRT fires/hotspots are generated within approximately 3 hours of satellite observation to meet the needs of the applications community.</p><p>- Standard data products are an internally consistent, well-calibrated record of the Earth’s geophysical properties to support science.</p><p>FIRMS maintains a limited history of NRT data to facilitate comparison between NRT and SP. For the majority of applications, FIRMS users do not need to concern themselves with the difference between NRT and Standard data. FIRMS will always make the \'best\' data available by default.</p>"}],"layer_refs":[{"id":"fires_landsat_landsat"},{"id":"fires_viirs_noaa20"},{"id":"fires_viirs_noaa21"},{"id":"fires_viirs_snpp"},{"id":"fires_modis_aqua"},{"id":"fires_modis_terra"},{"id":"fires_goes_g18"},{"id":"fires_goes_g18frp"},{"id":"fires_goes_g19"},{"id":"fires_goes_g19frp"},{"id":"fires_goes_Him9"},{"id":"fires_goes_Met9"},{"id":"fires_goes_Met10"},{"id":"fires_viirs_noaa20_nrt"},{"id":"fires_viirs_snpp_nrt"},{"id":"fires_modis_aqua_nrt"},{"id":"fires_modis_terra_nrt"},{"id":"fires_viirs_noaa20_sp"},{"id":"fires_viirs_snpp_sp"},{"id":"fires_modis_aqua_sp"},{"id":"fires_modis_terra_sp"}]},{"id":"advanced-fires-usfs","label":"Fires / Hotspots","menuLabel":"Advanced Fires / Hotspots Display","module":"advanced_fires_usfs","tag":"fires","menuDescription":"<p>Daily NRT/RT active fire detection data acquired by multiple polar-orbiting and geostationary satellite sensors. Includes user-defined legend options for visualizing active fire detection attributes (time since detection, detection confidence, fire radiative power).</p>","opened":true,"usePresetLayers":false,"localRedirect":"https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov","defaultLayers":["fires_modis_terra","fires_modis_aqua","fires_viirs_snpp","fires_viirs_noaa20","fires_viirs_noaa21","fires_landsat_landsat"],"groupBreakers":[{"id":"group1","label":"Polar Orbiting","opened":true,"layers":["fires_landsat_landsat","fires_viirs_noaa20","fires_viirs_noaa21","fires_viirs_snpp","fires_modis_aqua","fires_modis_terra"],"highlight":"recommended","menuDescription":"<p>Daily active fire detection data acquired by polar orbiting satellite sensors used in FIRMS. These satellites operate in a relatively low orbit and constantly circle the Earth passing over or near both poles while the planet rotates under them. Sensors on polar orbiting satellites  generally have swath widths ranging from 2,300km to 3,000km  providing two observations daily (daytime and nighttime) for most areas on the Earth\'s surface and provide data at relatively high spatial resolution.</p><p>Polar orbiting data are real-time (RT), within 1 hour, or near real-time (NRT) within 3 hours of satellite observation. Two daily observations are typically provided by each individual satellite sensor in the mid-latitudes (between 30 degrees north and south latitude). Additional observations are possible in the higher latitudes, up to 8 times near the poles, due to overlap of adjacent swaths.</p><p>Capabilities are provided to visualize these fire data based on their attributes. ‘Day’ and \'Night’ filters the data to display fires identified on daytime or nighttime overpasses. <br/><b>\'Simple\'</b> allows basic visualization of active fire data locations, <br/><b>\'Time-Based\'</b> displays fires coded by their time since detection relative to the present and <br/><b>\'Custom\'</b> allows active fire detection to be displayed by detection confidence or estimated fire radiative power.</p>"},{"id":"group2","label":"Geostationary","layers":["fires_goes_g18","fires_goes_g18frp","fires_goes_g19","fires_goes_g19frp"],"highlight":"beta","menuDescription":"<p>Active fire detection data derived from high temporal geostationary satellite sensors.</p><p>Daily RT active fire detection derived from geostationary satellite sensors. These satellites orbit at a very high altitude over a fixed point on the Earth\'s surface at the equator. They follow the daily rotation of the planet and the onboard sensors provide a full hemispheric view of the Earth centered on that fixed location at high temporal frequencies (multiple times per hour). The view geometry and curvature of the Earth result in distortion and geolocational accuracy effects in the imagery and active fire products with increasing distance in all directions from nadir.</p><p>Data are coarser spatial resolution than polar orbiting fire data, but acquired at intervals of 10-15 minutes and available within 30 minutes of observation. Data are highly filtered based on detection confidence to remove as many potential false positives as possible.</p><p>Capabilities are provided to visualize these fire data based on their attributes. ‘Day’ and \'Night’ filters the data to display fires identified on daytime or nighttime overpasses. \'Time Since Detection\' displays fires coded by their time since detection relative to the present. Non-GOES active fire data can also be displayed by detection confidence or estimated fire radiative power.</p>"},{"id":"group3","label":"NRT and Standard (for research)","layers":["fires_viirs_noaa20_nrt","fires_viirs_snpp_nrt","fires_modis_aqua_nrt","fires_modis_terra_nrt","fires_viirs_noaa20_sp","fires_viirs_snpp_sp","fires_modis_aqua_sp","fires_modis_terra_sp"],"menuDescription":"<p>This layer enables a comparison between Near Real-Time (NRT) and Standard fire products.</p><p>- NRT fires/hotspots are generated within approximately 3 hours of satellite observation to meet the needs of the applications community.</p><p>- Standard data products are an internally consistent, well-calibrated record of the Earth’s geophysical properties to support science.</p><p>FIRMS maintains a limited history of NRT data to facilitate comparison between NRT and SP. For the majority of applications, FIRMS users do not need to concern themselves with the difference between NRT and Standard data. FIRMS will always make the \'best\' data available by default.</p>"}],"layer_refs":[{"id":"fires_landsat_landsat"},{"id":"fires_viirs_noaa20"},{"id":"fires_viirs_noaa21"},{"id":"fires_viirs_snpp"},{"id":"fires_modis_aqua"},{"id":"fires_modis_terra"},{"id":"fires_goes_g18"},{"id":"fires_goes_g18frp"},{"id":"fires_goes_g19"},{"id":"fires_goes_g19frp"},{"id":"fires_viirs_noaa20_nrt"},{"id":"fires_viirs_snpp_nrt"},{"id":"fires_modis_aqua_nrt"},{"id":"fires_modis_terra_nrt"},{"id":"fires_viirs_noaa20_sp"},{"id":"fires_viirs_snpp_sp"},{"id":"fires_modis_aqua_sp"},{"id":"fires_modis_terra_sp"}]},{"id":"ba-fires","label":"Fires / Hotspots","menuLabel":"Advanced Fires / Hotspots Display for Burned Area","menuDescription":"<p>Daily NRT/RT active fire detection data acquired by multiple polar-orbiting satellite sensors used in FIRMS.</p><p>Detection data provide context to available burned area data, such as identifying the first active fire detection for a particular burned area. Additional user-defined legend options for visualizing active fire detection attributes (detection confidence, fire radiative power).</p>","module":"ba_fires","tag":"fires","usePresetLayers":false,"localRedirect":"https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov","groupBreakers":[{"id":"group1","label":"Polar Orbiting","opened":true,"layers":["fires_landsat_landsat","fires_viirs_noaa20","fires_viirs_noaa21","fires_viirs_snpp","fires_modis_aqua","fires_modis_terra"],"menuDescription":"<p>Daily active fire detection data acquired by polar orbiting satellite sensors used in FIRMS. These satellites operate in a relatively low orbit and constantly circle the Earth passing over or near both poles while the planet rotates under them. Sensors on polar orbiting satellites  generally have swath widths ranging from 2,300km to 3,000km  providing two observations daily (daytime and nighttime) for most areas on the Earth\'s surface and provide data at relatively high spatial resolution.</p><p>Polar orbiting data are real-time (RT) or near real-time (NRT), provided within 1 hour and 3 hours of satellite observation, respectively. Two daily observations daily are typically provided by each individual satellite sensor in the mid-latitudes (between 30 degrees north and south latitude). Additional observations are possible in the higher latitudes, up to 8 times near the poles, due to overlap of adjacent swaths.</p>"}],"layer_refs":[{"id":"fires_landsat_landsat"},{"id":"fires_viirs_noaa20"},{"id":"fires_viirs_noaa21"},{"id":"fires_viirs_snpp"},{"id":"fires_modis_aqua"},{"id":"fires_modis_terra"}]},{"id":"smoke-fires","label":"Fires / Hotspots","menuLabel":"Advanced Fires / Hotspots Display for Burned Area","menuDescription":"<p>Daily NRT/RT active fire detection data acquired by multiple polar-orbiting satellite sensors. Additional user-defined legend options for visualizing active fire detection attributes (detection confidence, fire radiative power).</p><p>Fire detections are helpful to identify the locations of wildland fire sources of smoke which can be occluded by smoke, etc. in the imagery.</p>","module":"smoke_fires","tag":"fires","usePresetLayers":false,"localRedirect":"https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov","defaultLayers":["fires_landsat_landsat","fires_viirs_noaa20","fires_viirs_noaa21","fires_viirs_snpp","fires_modis_aqua","fires_modis_terra"],"groupBreakers":[{"id":"group1","label":"Polar Orbiting","opened":true,"layers":["fires_landsat_landsat","fires_viirs_noaa20","fires_viirs_noaa21","fires_viirs_snpp","fires_modis_aqua","fires_modis_terra"],"menuDescription":"<p>Daily active fire detection data acquired by polar orbiting satellite sensors used in FIRMS. These satellites operate in a relatively low orbit and constantly circle the Earth passing over or near both poles while the planet rotates under them. Sensors on polar orbiting satellites  generally have swath widths ranging from 2,300km to 3,000km  providing two observations daily (daytime and nighttime) for most areas on the Earth\'s surface and provide data at relatively high spatial resolution.</p><p>Polar orbiting data are real-time (RT) or near real-time (NRT), provided within 1 hour and 3 hours of satellite observation, respectively. Two daily observations daily are typically provided by each individual satellite sensor in the mid-latitudes (between 30 degrees north and south latitude). Additional observations are possible in the higher latitudes, up to 8 times near the poles, due to overlap of adjacent swaths.</p>"}],"layer_refs":[{"id":"fires_landsat_landsat"},{"id":"fires_viirs_noaa20"},{"id":"fires_viirs_noaa21"},{"id":"fires_viirs_snpp"},{"id":"fires_modis_aqua"},{"id":"fires_modis_terra"}]},{"id":"burned-area2","label":"MODIS Burned Area","menuLabel":"MODIS Burned Area","menuDescription":"<p>NASA standard science product providing identification of areas burned based on analysis of surface reflectance imagery and active fire data. Product is available with approximately a 5 month lag.</p><p>The MODIS burned area data is compiled and distributed as monthly products. To produce it, a continuous month of daily surface reflectance imagery and active fire detection data are required in addition to the 10 days prior and after the target month. This production requirement and other factors contribute to the timeliness of this data product. To visualize recent burns using current data, the NRT false color composite imagery from MODIS and VIIRS is ideal for identifying large burn scars. Additionally, higher resolution HLS false color composite imagery can also be used.</p>","module":"burned_area2","tag":"burned_area","openedOnVisible":true,"opened":true,"localRedirect":"https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov","groupBreakers":[{"id":"ba_group1","label":"#YEAR#","type":"monthly","opened":true,"layers":["modis_ba_1","modis_ba_2","modis_ba_3","modis_ba_4","modis_ba_5","modis_ba_6","modis_ba_7","modis_ba_8","modis_ba_9","modis_ba_10","modis_ba_11","modis_ba_12"]},{"id":"ba_group2","label":"#YEAR#","type":"monthly","layers":["modis_ba2_1","modis_ba2_2","modis_ba2_3","modis_ba2_4","modis_ba2_5","modis_ba2_6","modis_ba2_7","modis_ba2_8","modis_ba2_9","modis_ba2_10","modis_ba2_11","modis_ba2_12"]},{"id":"ba_group3","label":"#YEAR#","type":"monthly","layers":["modis_ba3_1","modis_ba3_2","modis_ba3_3","modis_ba3_4","modis_ba3_5","modis_ba3_6","modis_ba3_7","modis_ba3_8","modis_ba3_9","modis_ba3_10","modis_ba3_11","modis_ba3_12"]},{"id":"ba_group4","label":"#YEAR#","type":"yearly","layers":["modis_yba"]},{"id":"ba_group5","label":"#YEAR#","type":"yearly","layers":["modis_yba_2"]},{"id":"ba_group6","label":"#YEAR#","type":"yearly","layers":["modis_yba_3"]}]},{"id":"burned-area-viirs","label":"VIIRS Burned Area","menuLabel":"VIIRS Burned Area","menuDescription":"<p>NASA standard science product providing identification of areas burned based on analysis of surface reflectance imagery and active fire data. Product is available with approximately a 5 month lag.</p><p>The VIIRS burned area data is compiled and distributed as monthly products. To produce it, a continuous month of daily surface reflectance imagery and active fire detection data are required in addition to the 10 days prior and after the target month. This production requirement and other factors contribute to the timeliness of this data product. To visualize recent burns using current data, the NRT false color composite imagery from MODIS and VIIRS is ideal for identifying large burn scars. Additionally, higher resolution HLS false color composite imagery can also be used.</p>","module":"burned_area_viirs","tag":"burned_area_viirs","openedOnVisible":true,"opened":true,"localRedirect":"https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov","groupBreakers":[{"id":"ba_viirs_group1","label":"#YEAR#","type":"monthly","opened":true,"layers":["viirs_ba_1","viirs_ba_2","viirs_ba_3","viirs_ba_4","viirs_ba_5","viirs_ba_6","viirs_ba_7","viirs_ba_8","viirs_ba_9","viirs_ba_10","viirs_ba_11","viirs_ba_12"]},{"id":"ba_viirs_group2","label":"#YEAR#","type":"monthly","layers":["viirs_ba2_1","viirs_ba2_2","viirs_ba2_3","viirs_ba2_4","viirs_ba2_5","viirs_ba2_6","viirs_ba2_7","viirs_ba2_8","viirs_ba2_9","viirs_ba2_10","viirs_ba2_11","viirs_ba2_12"]},{"id":"ba_viirs_group3","label":"#YEAR#","type":"monthly","layers":["viirs_ba3_1","viirs_ba3_2","viirs_ba3_3","viirs_ba3_4","viirs_ba3_5","viirs_ba3_6","viirs_ba3_7","viirs_ba3_8","viirs_ba3_9","viirs_ba3_10","viirs_ba3_11","viirs_ba3_12"]},{"id":"ba_viirs_group4","label":"#YEAR#","type":"yearly","layers":["viirs_yba"]},{"id":"ba_viirs_group5","label":"#YEAR#","type":"yearly","layers":["viirs_yba_2"]},{"id":"ba_viirs_group6","label":"#YEAR#","type":"yearly","layers":["viirs_yba_3"]}]},{"id":"basemaps-basic","label":"Static Backgrounds","menuLabel":"Basic Static Backgrounds","menuDescription":"<p>Static imagery and base map backgrounds for providing context to the satellite imagery, fire/hotspot data and other satellite data products.</p>","module":"basemaps","tag":"basemap","opened":true,"defaultLayers":["earth"],"layer_refs":[{"id":"earth"},{"id":"firefly"},{"id":"street"},{"id":"topo"}]},{"id":"basemaps-advanced","label":"Static Backgrounds","menuLabel":"Advanced Static Backgrounds","menuDescription":"<p>Static imagery and base map backgrounds for providing context to the satellite imagery, fire/hotspot data and other satellite data products.</p>","defaultLayers":["earth"],"opened":true,"useLayerRefsOrder":true,"tag":"basemap","module":"basemaps"},{"id":"fire-grids","label":"Gridded Fire Hotspots (Daily)","menuLabel":"Gridded Fire Hotspots","menuDescription":"<p>Gridded daily fire hotspots grouped into 1/4 x 1/4 degree.</p>","tag":"fire-grids","module":"fire-grids","localRedirect":"https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov"},{"id":"overlays-basic","label":"Overlays","menuLabel":"Basic Overlay Layers","menuDescription":"<p>Contextual geospatial data layers (geopolitical boundaries, administrative boundaries, etc.).</p>","module":"basic","defaultLayers":["country-outline"],"tag":"overlay","layer_refs":[{"id":"countries"},{"id":"country-outline"},{"id":"volcanoes"},{"id":"protected_areas_regional"},{"id":"protected_areas"}]},{"id":"overlays","label":"Overlays","tag":"overlay","menuLabel":"Advanced Overlay Layers","menuDescription":"<p>Contextual geospatial data layers (geopolitical boundaries, administrative boundaries, populated places/built up areas, protected areas, etc.).</p>","defaultLayers":["country-outline"],"module":"basic"},{"id":"overlays-basic-usfs","label":"Overlays","menuLabel":"Basic Overlay Layers","menuDescription":"<p>Contextual geospatial data layers (geopolitical boundaries, administrative boundaries, etc.).</p>","module":"basic","defaultLayers":["countries"],"tag":"overlay","layer_refs":[{"id":"countries"},{"id":"country-outline"},{"id":"protected_areas"}]},{"id":"overlays-usfs","label":"Overlays","tag":"overlay","menuLabel":"Advanced Overlay Layers","menuDescription":"<p>Contextual geospatial data layers (geopolitical boundaries, administrative boundaries, populated places/built up areas, protected areas, etc.).</p>","defaultLayers":["countries"],"module":"basic"},{"id":"active-alerts-basic","label":"Active Alerts","tag":"alerts","opened":true,"menuLabel":"Active Alerts","module":"basic","menuDescription":"<p>Daily active wildland fire/incident locations, reporting information and perimeters as well as fire weather advisories.</p><p>US wildland fire data is provided by individual incidents, Geographic Area Coordination Centers and the National Interagency Fire Center. US fire weather advisories provided by the NOAA National Weather Service.</p><p>Canadian data is provided by provincial and territorial wildland fire management agencies and NRCan.</p>","defaultLayers":["active-usa","active-ca","fire-perimeter"],"layer_refs":[{"id":"active-fire-label"},{"id":"active-usa"},{"id":"active-ca"},{"id":"fire-perimeter"},{"id":"noaa-fire-watch"},{"id":"noaa-red-flag"}]},{"id":"active-alerts-advanced","label":"Active Alerts","tag":"alerts","opened":true,"menuLabel":"Active Alerts","menuDescription":"<p>Daily active wildland fire/incident locations, reporting information and perimeters as well as fire weather advisories.</p><p>US wildland fire data is provided by individual incidents, Geographic Area Coordination Centers and the National Interagency Fire Center. US fire weather advisories provided by the NOAA National Weather Service.</p><p>Canadian data is provided by provincial and territorial wildland fire management agencies and NRCan.</p>","defaultLayers":["active-usa","active-ca","fire-perimeter"],"module":"basic"},{"id":"smoke","label":"Smoke and Aerosols","tag":"smoke","menuLabel":"Smoke Layers","usePresetLayers":false,"useLayerRefsOrder":true,"menuDescription":"<p>Daily NRT aerosol products that measure the concentration (thickness and height) and other properties of smoke and dust in the atmospheric column that could reduce visibility or impact human health.</p><p>The OMPS Aerosol Index layer measures the concentration (thickness and height) of smoke and dust in the atmospheric column. It is helpful for identifying smoke, its relative concentration levels and its migration. The OMPS PyroCB (or PyroCumulonimbus) Index layer is a variant of the standard OMPS Aerosol Index adapted to measure relatively dense aerosol concentrations higher in the atmosphere.</p>","defaultLayers":["snpp_omps_aerosol"],"module":"basic"},{"id":"smoke-basic","label":"Smoke and Aerosols","tag":"smoke","usePresetLayers":false,"useLayerRefsOrder":true,"menuLabel":"Smoke Layers for Advanced mode","menuDescription":"<p>Daily NRT aerosol products that measure the concentration (thickness and height) and other properties of smoke and dust in the atmospheric column that could reduce visibility or impact human health.</p><p>The OMPS Aerosol Index layer measures the concentration (thickness and height) of smoke and dust in the atmospheric column. It is helpful for identifying smoke, its relative concentration levels and its migration. The OMPS PyroCB (or PyroCumulonimbus) Index layer is a variant of the standard OMPS Aerosol Index adapted to measure relatively dense aerosol concentrations higher in the atmosphere.</p><p>NRT true color composite imagery from MODIS and VIIRS is also useful for identifying the current extent of smoke/aerosols for each individual satellite pass and monitor its change in extent and migration between daily satellite passes.</p>","module":"basic"},{"id":"harmonized-advanced","label":"Harmonized Landsat Sentinel-2 Imagery","tag":"hls","openedOnVisible":true,"menuLabel":"Harmonized Landsat Sentinel Imagery","menuDescription":"<p>Daily Landsat and Sentinel-2 true color and false color composite imagery. Available 2-4 days after satellite overpass.</p><p>HLS imagery is relatively high resolution imagery (30m) derived from daily Landsat OLI and Sentinel-2 MSI observations. However, it is available 2-4 days after satellite overpass and has a lower frequent observation frequency. Landsat and Sentinel-2 typically acquire imagery for a given location every 8 days and 4-5 days, respectively.</p><p><b>\'True color\'</b> imagery (so called because this combination of wavelengths is similar to what the human eye would see) is displayed when the timeline is adjusted to coincide with available imagery. \'False color\' imagery is only displayed when zoomed to a large scale (zoom scale of 9 or higher). \'True color\' imagery should be used to locate the users’ area of interest. When sufficiently zoomed in, the \'false color\' imagery can be displayed.</p><p><b>\'False color\'</b> imagery enables us to visualization wavelengths that the human eye cannot see (i.e. short wave infrared).In the \'false color\' imagery, burned areas appear almost brick red making it easier to see fire affected areas and water is very dark, almost black, which makes it much easier to see lakes or flooded lands.</p>","module":"basic","groupCombine":[{"id":"group1","options":[{"label":"Landsat 8/9 and Sentinel 2","directive":[0,1],"infoLabel":"HLS L30 / HLS S30"},{"label":"Landsat 8/9","directive":[1],"infoLabel":"HLS L30"},{"label":"Sentinel 2","directive":[0],"infoLabel":"HLS S30"}],"groups":[{"layers":["hlss30-true-color","hlsl30-true-color"]},{"layers":["hlss30-false-color","hlsl30-false-color"]},{"layers":["hlss30-ndvi","hlsl30-ndvi"]},{"layers":["hlss30-ndmi","hlsl30-ndmi"]},{"layers":["hlss30-nbr","hlsl30-nbr"]},{"layers":["hlss30-nbr2","hlsl30-nbr2"]}]}],"groupBreakers":[{"id":"group1","label":"Customize HLS Bands","opened":false,"disableAll":true,"class":"hls_gb","layers":["hlss30-custom","hlsl30-custom"],"menuDescription":"<p>Customize HLS bands.</p>"}]},{"id":"feds_exp","label":"Fire Perimeter","menuLabel":"Experimental Fire Perimeter Layers","menuDescription":"Layer still in testing","module":"basic","bubble":"experimental","bubbleClass":"bubble_experimental","defaultLayers":["eis_fire_lf_perimeter_nrt"],"tag":"eis","opened":true,"layer_refs":[{"id":"eis_fire_lf_perimeter_nrt"}]},{"id":"persistent_fires","label":"Static Thermal Anomalies","menuLabel":"Static Thermal Anomalies","menuDescription":"Potential contributors to static thermal anomalies","module":"basic","useLayerRefsOrder":true,"tag":"pf","groupBreakers":[{"id":"group1","label":"Industrial Plants","opened":false,"layers":["cement_plants","gas_flares","gi_steel_plants","mineral_resources","nonferrous_metal","petro_chemical","steel_plants"],"menuDescription":"<p>Collection of layers depicting industrial activity. Individual layers provide additional information about particular dataset.</p>"},{"id":"group2","label":"Power Plants","opened":false,"layers":["power_biomass","power_coal","power_gas","power_geothermal","power_hydro","power_oil","power_other","power_solar","power_storage","power_wind"],"menuDescription":"<p>Collection of layers depicting location of power plants.</p>"}]},{"id":"identify","label":"Identify","menuLabel":"Identify Layers","menuDescription":"Supporting layers for identify","module":"identify","noGroup":true,"opened":false,"layer_refs":[{"id":"identify"}]},{"id":"dynamic-basic","label":"Dynamic Imagery","module":"basic","tag":"imagery","menuLabel":"Basic Dynamic Imagery","menuDescription":"<p>Daily daytime NRT True Color satellite imagery from polar orbiting satellite sensors. Useful for identifying cloud and smoke which may obscure fire detections.</p>","opened":true,"isMultiLayerActive":false,"layer_refs":[{"id":"noaa20_crtc"},{"id":"noaa21_crtc"},{"id":"viirs_crtc"},{"id":"aqua_crc"},{"id":"terra_crc"}]},{"id":"dynamic-advanced","label":"Dynamic Imagery","menuLabel":"Advanced Dynamic Imagery","menuDescription":"<p>Daily NRT true color and false color imagery from polar-orbiting satellite sensors used in FIRMS and RT imagery from geostationary satellite sensors (currently GOES ABI only).</p>","opened":true,"hasMultiLayer":true,"tag":"imagery","module":"basic"},{"id":"dynamic-smoke","label":"Dynamic Imagery","menuLabel":"Daily NRT true color and false color imagery from polar-orbiting satellite sensors used in FIRMS and RT imagery from geostationary satellite sensors (currently GOES ABI only).","menuDescription":"<p>Daily, NRT true color imagery for identifying smoke and clouds and are provided at multiple times during the day.<br/>NRT true color composite imagery from MODIS and VIIRS is also useful for identifying the current extent of smoke/aerosols for each individual satellite pass and monitor its change in extent and migration between daily satellite passes.</p>","opened":true,"hasMultiLayer":true,"tag":"imagery","module":"basic","layer_refs":[{"id":"goes_w_color"},{"id":"goes_e_color"},{"id":"noaa20_crtc"},{"id":"noaa21_crtc"},{"id":"viirs_crtc"},{"id":"aqua_crc"},{"id":"terra_crc"}]},{"id":"dynamic-ba","label":"Dynamic Imagery","module":"basic","tag":"imagery","menuLabel":"Daily NRT false color from polar orbiting satellite sensors used in FIRMS.","menuDescription":"<p>Daily NRT false color imagery from polar orbiting satellite sensors used in FIRMS. False color imagery enables visualization of daily extent and growth of burned areas.</p>","opened":true,"hasMultiLayer":true,"layer_refs":[{"id":"terra_srb721"},{"id":"aqua_srb721"},{"id":"viirs_m11"},{"id":"noaa20_m11"},{"id":"noaa21_m11"}]},{"id":"orbits","label":"Orbit Tracks and Overpass Times","menuLabel":"Orbit Tracks and Overpass Times","menuDescription":"<p>Daily orbit tracks and overpass times for polar-orbiting satellites included in FIRMS.</p><p>Polar orbiting satellites circle the Earth at or near the poles while the planet rotates below. Each orbit takes approximately 100 minutes to complete with daytime and nighttime observations conducted on each orbit. The provided orbit tracks represent the path followed by the satellite on the selected date with the associated times indicating the time of overpass for that location in UTC. Polar orbiting satellites do not follow the same track every day. Daily tracks are different and typically follow a cycle that is repeated every 16 days.</p><p>Orbit track data are used to identify the anticipated time of satellite overpass for particular geographic areas for the current date. Tracks for previous dates can also be visualized to support retrospective analyses. The anticipated number of daily observations for a specific location can be determined by using one of several NASA satellite overpass predictors. FIRMS recommends the NASA Ocean Color Overpass Predictor. To view the current location of satellites, tools such as <a href=\'https://in-the-sky.org/satmap_worldmap.php\' class=\'external\' target=\'_blank\' rel=\'noopener noreferrer\'>Live Map of Satellite Positions</a> and <a href=\'https://www.n2yo.com/\' class=\'external\' target=\'_blank\' rel=\'noopener noreferrer\'>N2YO</a> are available.</p>","opened":false,"tag":"orbits","openedOnVisible":true,"module":"basic"},{"id":"orbits-smoke","label":"Orbit Tracks and Overpass Times","menuLabel":"Orbit Tracks and Overpass Times","menuDescription":"<p>Daily orbit tracks and overpass times for polar-orbiting satellites included in FIRMS.</p><p>Polar orbiting satellites circle the Earth at or near the poles while the planet rotates below. Each orbit takes approximately 100 minutes to complete with daytime and nighttime obser